WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.440
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:10.290
This piece of work is not meant to be a highly
useful application, only a tongue in cheek

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:15.750
jab at the rising trend of trying to solve
simple problems using deep learning without

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:18.390
carefully examining the problem at hand.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:24.740
As always, we note that all intuitive explanations
are wrong, but some are helpful, and the most

00:00:24.740 --> 00:00:29.140
precise way to express these thoughts can
be done by using mathematics.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.220
However, we shall leave that to the textbooks
and will try to understand these concepts

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:37.620
by floating about on the wings of intuition.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:44.500
In mathematics, a matrix is a rectangular
array in which we can store numbers and symbols.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:50.730
Matrices can be interpreted in many ways,
for instance, we can think of them as transformations.

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:57.020
Multiplying a matrix with a vector means applying
this transform to the vector, such as scaling,

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:58.530
rotation or shearing.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:02.710
The rank of a matrix can be intuitively explained
in many ways.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:08.600
My favorite intuition is that the rank encodes
the information content of the matrix.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:13.440
For instance, in an earlier work on Separable
Subsurface Scattering, we recognized that

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.970
many of these matrices that encode light scattering
inside translucent materials, are of relatively

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:21.040
low rank.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:26.700
This means that the information within is
highly structured and it is not random noise.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:32.270
And from this low rank property follows that
we can compress and represent this phenomenon

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:37.590
using simpler data structures, leading to
an extremely efficient algorithm to simulate

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.750
light scattering within our skin.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:45.620
However, the main point is that finding out
the rank of a large matrix is an expensive

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:46.620
operation.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:51.570
It is also important to note that we can also
visualize these matrices by mapping the numbers

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.340
within to different colors.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:59.500
As a fun sidenote, the paper finds, that the
uglier the colorscheme is, the better suited

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.250
it is for learning.

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:07.000
This way, after computing the ranks of many
matrices, we can create a lot of input images

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.670
and output ranks for the neural network to
learn on.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:15.840
After that, the goal is that we feed in an
unknown matrix in the form of an image, and

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.190
the network would have to guess what the rank
is.

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:24.920
It is almost like having an expert scientist
unleash his intuition on such a matrix, much

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.310
like a fun guessing game for intoxicated mathematicians.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:34.090
And the ultimate question, as always is, how
does this knowledge learned by the neural

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.020
network generalize?

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:41.720
The results are decent, but not spectacular,
but they also offer some insights as to which

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.840
matrices have surprising ranks.

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:50.099
We can also try computing the products of
matrices, which intuitively translates to

00:02:50.099 --> 00:02:55.200
guessing the result after we have done one
transformation after the other.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.310
Like the output of scaling after a rotation
operation.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:05.010
They also tried to compute the inverse of
matrices, for which the intuition can be undoing

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.550
the transformation.

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:11.810
If it is a rotation to a given direction,
the inverse would be rotating back the exact

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:18.000
same amount, or if we scaled something up,
then scaling it back down would be its inverse.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.190
Of course, these are not the only operations
that we can do with matrices, we only used

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.640
these for the sake of demonstration.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:31.830
The lead author states on his website that
this paper shows that "linear algebra can

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:35.459
be replaced with machine learning".

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.129
Talk about being funny and tongue in cheek.

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:43.310
Also, I have linked the website of David in
the description box, he has a lot of great

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:49.750
works and I am surely not doing him justice
by of all those great works, covering this

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:50.750
one.

00:03:50.750 --> 00:03:57.400
Rufus von Woofles, graduate of the prestigious
Muddy Paws University was the third author

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:02.569
of the paper, overlooking the entirety of
the work and making sure that the quality

00:04:02.569 --> 00:04:05.040
of the results is impeccable.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:11.280
As future work, I would propose replacing
the basic mathematical operators such as addition

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.230
and multiplication by machine learning.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:20.690
Except that it is already done and is hilariously
fun, and it even supports division by zero.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:23.879
Talk about the almighty powers of deep learning.

00:04:23.879 --> 00:04:28.119
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

